r it that's also common it's very  common that when you get a proposal in  the House or Senate the proposal says  what you want to do you separately  outline how it would be paid for in an  appropriations bill  it's a fair critique but that's commonly  how these things work  the most important thing about this  document is that with the Democratic  primary ramping up no 2020 Democrat has  straight-up backed away from it you do  have some Democrats paint playing a  little coy with it like Elizabeth Warren  said she's into it but when she was  asked what specifically do you support  in it she didn't say cory booker i  forget exactly what he said about it but  it was again this kind of more timid  support of it but if you if at this  point you're running in 2020 is a  democrat and you say i am against the  bill that's a real problem and that's a  good thing and that's one of the most  important things that this bill that  this proposal actually does now we do  need to figure out a path to doing it  and that's a really important thing and  what we're already running into is that  you have a number of people on the Left  making strategic mistakes like for  example i saw an adviser to alexandria  Cascio cortez on the tucker carlson show  I apologize that I forget his name I  believe is a Cornell University law  professor and a lot of the defenses that  he issued were arguing that Tucker had  merely misunderstood the proposal and  what aoc tweeted and said about it and  some of the denials were really not  particularly believable I think that  that's a mistake  I think the idea that that would much  better serve the left would be to be  completely on the same page that listen  it doesn't say how we pay for it that's  normal  separately we're going to put out a  proposal for how to pay for it that's  what Bernie did with Medicare for all  and then with how to pay for it when  they say a lot of these things are too  ambitious you respond and you say these  are the goals let'